{"entries":{"chapter-48-study-elder-mistreatment|Chapter 48 Study: Elder Mistreatment":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Mistreatment includes physical, sexual or psychological abuse, neglect, abandonment and financial exploitation; definitions vary (p719).":["u3"],"copy|It may occur at home, in the community or in institutions, often within a relationship of trust (p719).":["u4"],"copy|Studies suggest as many as 10% of community-dwelling older adults are affected annually; smaller studies suggest nearly 50% among people with dementia (p720).":["u5"],"copy|Research suggests authorities identify as few as 1 in 24 cases. Fear, dependence, isolation or cognitive impairment may prevent disclosure (p720).":["u6"],"copy|Risk factors are neither a diagnosis nor a reliable way to exclude mistreatment; family circumstances and perpetrator–victim relationships matter (p720–721).":["u7"],"copy|Chronic disease may mimic abuse, and abuse may be misattributed to chronic disease (p722).":["u8"],"copy|Maintain clinical suspicion; examine injuries, hygiene, nutrition, medications, function, cognition and social circumstances (p722–724).":["u9"],"copy|The suspected perpetrator's presence can complicate assessment; obtain appropriate expertise and collateral information sensitively (p722–723).":["u10"],"copy|Establish whether there is immediate danger, then make a practical safety plan even when danger is not immediate (p723–724).":["u11"],"copy|In most U.S. states, reporting to adult protective services is required; available services and statutory arrangements vary (p724).":["u12"],"copy|If the patient lacks capacity, document objective evidence; guardianship may be needed with protective/community services (p724–725).":["u13"],"copy|A capable person may choose to remain in an abusive environment: explain escalation risk, review safety options and remain available (p725).":["u14"],"copy|Use individualized, trauma-informed management. The chapter reports no good-quality randomized trials establishing an intervention strategy (p723, p725).":["u15"],"copy|Consider neglect and failures of care planning, as well as staff abuse (p725).":["u16"],"copy|Resident-to-resident verbal, physical and sexual mistreatment also occurs; institutional safeguards do not eliminate the need for vigilance (p726).":["u17"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u18"],"copy|CHAPTER 48s / HAZZARD":["u19"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 48; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u20"],"copy|Recognize the spectrum":["u21"],"copy|Clinical assessment":["u22"],"copy|Safety & response":["u23"],"copy|Institutional settings":["u24"],"copy|No past-exam questions are currently linked to this chapter.":["u25"],"copy|Three past-exam questions link to this topic through the Israeli procedure, and June 2026 Q28 also cites Chapter 15, page 213.":["u25"]},"maxId":25}
